      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 2 of 4 of an OIML certificate markdown. The certificate is R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1, issued by Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM). It covers the QALCOSONIC W1 water meter (R49 - water meters).\n\nContent in this fragment:\n\n1. Software versions table:\n   - Software version 1.03, checksum C983, remarks DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\n   - Software version 2.02, checksum CD00, remarks DN40\n\n2. Section 2.5 Accountable alarms — free text about the calculator/indication device detecting faults.\n\n3. Section 3: Technical and metrological data — Tab. 1 and Tab. 2.\n\nTab. 1: QALCOSONIC W1, DN15:\n- Nominal diameter DN: 15 mm\n- Permanent flowrate Q3: m³/h — 1.6 (5 columns) and 2.5 (5 columns)\n- Minimum flowrate Q1: m³/h — 0.020, 0.010, 0.0064, 0.005, 0.004 | 0.031, 0.0156, 0.010, 0.0062, 0.0031\n- Transitional flowrate Q2: m³/h — 0.032, 0.016, 0.010, 0.008, 0.0064 | 0.050, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010, 0.005\n- Overload flowrate Q4: m³/h — 2 | 3.125\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: -80, 160, 250, 315, 400 | 80, 160, 250, 400, 800\n- Ratio Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Connection thread: G ¾\n- Construction length L: mm — 80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170 (list of values)\n- Installation position: Horizontal / Vertical\n- Water temperature range: °C — 0.1 to 30, 0.1 to 50, 30 to 70, 0.1 to 70 (multiple options)\n- Meter temperature class: T30, T50, T30/70, T70\n- Maximum working pressure MAP: bar — 16\n- Pressure loss class ΔP: bar — 0.16 (for Q3=1.6) | 0.25 (for Q3=2.5)\n- MPE upper flowrates: ±2 (at Θ ≤ 30°C), ±3 (at Θ > 30°C)\n- MPE lower flowrates: ±5\n- Scale interval: m³ — 0.001\n- Capacity of calculator: m³ — 999999.999\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Mechanical class: M1\n- Climatic class: -15 to +70 °C\n- Electromagnetic class: E2\n- Environmental classification: B/O\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0D0\n- Battery: li-battery 3.6 V, life time 16 years\n\nTab. 2: QALCOSONIC W1, DN20:\n- Nominal diameter DN: 20 mm\n- Permanent flowrate Q3: 2.5 (4 columns) | 4 (5 columns)\n- Minimum flowrate Q1: 0.031, 0.0156, 0.010, 0.0062 | 0.050, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010, 0.005\n- Transitional flowrate Q2: 0.05, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010 | 0.080, 0.040, 0.026, 0.016, 0.008\n- Overload flowrate Q4: 3.125 | 5\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 80, 160, 250, 400 | 80, 160, 250, 400, 800\n- Ratio Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Connection thread: G 1\n- Construction length L: 105, 110, 130, 165, 190\n- Installation position: Horizontal / Vertical\n- Water temperature range: 0.1 to 30, 0.1 to 50, 30 to 70, 0.1 to 70\n- Meter temperature class: T30, T50, T30/70, T70\n- Maximum working pressure: 16 bar\n- Pressure loss ΔP: 0.16 (Q3=2.5) | 0.25 (Q3=4)\n- MPE upper: ±2 (at Θ≤30°C), ±3 (at Θ>30°C)\n- MPE lower: ±5\n- Scale interval: 0.001 m³\n- Capacity of calculator: 999999.999 m³\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Mechanical class: M1\n- Climatic class: -15 to +70 °C\n- Electromagnetic class: E2\n- Environmental classification: B/O\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0D0\n- Battery: li-battery 3.6 V, life time 16 years\n\nThe certificate number: R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1. Also TK-P05-85 — Page 4 of 12 — V03/23-01-10 (this is a form/project number for the Slovak authority).\n\nSince this is a fragment of a larger certificate (part 2 of 4), I should extract only what's in this fragment. The certificate-level info available here: certificate number R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1. The recommendation R49, edition 2013. Actually the \"2013\" in the certificate number refers to the edition of R49. Scheme is A (the \"-A-\" in the number). Let me include:\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- page_total: 12 (from \"Page 4 of 12 pages\" — total pages 12). This appears in the fragment, so I can include page_total: 12.\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present? The stamp says SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY. No ID number given. Omit.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\" (from cert number R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02)\n- edition: 2013\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [2] — well, accuracy class 2 for water meters under R49... R49 has accuracy classes. The table says Accuracy class 2. So accuracy_classes: [\"2\"] or [2]. The schema says accuracy_classes[]. I'll use [\"2\"].\n\nHmm, but should I include recommendation? The rule says extract content present in THIS fragment only. The certificate number mentions R49/2013, so yes.\n\nSoftware table: This fits matrix_tables or components. The software versions apply per DN: version 1.03 for DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32; version 2.02 for DN40. This could go into matrix_tables with name \"Software versions\", columns [\"software_version\", \"checksum\", \"remarks\"], rows.\n\nModel family: This cert covers multiple variants — the tables vary by DN (nominal diameter) and by Q3 ratio. The models are:\n- DN15: Q3=1.6 (ratios 80, 160, 250, 315, 400) and Q3=2.5 (ratios 80, 160, 250, 400, 800)\n- DN20: Q3=2.5 (ratios 80, 160, 250, 400) and Q3=4 (ratios 80, 160, 250, 400, 800)\n\nBut parts 3-4 presumably have DN25, DN32, DN40 tables. Since we only have this fragment, I should model what's here. The model family is QALCOSONIC W1. Models vary by DN. Within each DN, there are variants by Q3 and by ratio Q3/Q1.\n\nHow to structure the model_level characteristics? The main varying axis across tables is DN. Within a DN table, values vary by Q3 variant (two sub-columns) and by ratio.\n\nI think the best approach given the schema:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"QALCOSONIC W1\" (actually QALCOSONIC W1 is the type designation; family name could be that)\n- models: from this fragment, the identified variants are DN15 (with Q3=1.6 and Q3=2.5) and DN20 (with Q3=2.5 and Q3=4). Hmm, but that's a lot of combinations.\n\nAlternatively, model_id per DN: \"DN15\", \"DN20\" — and treat Q3 variant as config-level? Actually within each DN there are two Q3 variants, and within each Q3 variant, there are 5 ratio variants (each ratio implies a different Q1/Q2).\n\nThe cleanest representation per the R137 example: matrix_tables preserve the tables as-is, and model_level gets the key varying attributes.\n\nGiven the instruction \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" via matrix_tables, and populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level with key attributes.\n\nI'll do:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"QALCOSONIC W1\"\n- models: [\n  {\"model_id\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN15 Q3=2.5\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN20 Q3=2.5\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN20 Q3=4\"}\n]\n\nHmm, but maybe simpler: models keyed by DN: DN15, DN20. But then Q3 varies within DN. The tab structure suggests: for each DN, there are two flowrate variants distinguished by Q3.\n\nActually let me reconsider. Within a DN, the two variants (Q3=1.6/2.5 for DN15) each contain 5 ratio options. Each ratio option has its own Q1, Q2. So the full model space is: DN × Q3-variant × ratio. That's 4 DN-values × ~2 Q3 × 5 ratios = 40 variants across the whole cert.\n\nFor this fragment, I'll use model_id like \"DN15/Q3=1.6/ratio80\" etc.? That's 20 models for this fragment. That might be overkill but it's the most faithful.\n\nAlternative: model_level entries with matrix style — attribute \"minimum_flowrate_q1\" with values per model where model = \"DN15 Q3=1.6 R80\" etc.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's most useful and follows the schema. The schema example for R137: models = size values (G1.6, G2.5), model_level attributes qmax/qt/qmin with values per model. The analog here: models = DN15, DN20 (and later DN25, DN32, DN40 in other fragments). Then attributes like q3, q4, construction_length, connection_thread vary per model.\n\nBut Q3 varies within DN... Actually for DN15, Q3 can be 1.6 OR 2.5. So Q3 is itself a model-level attribute with two values per DN. If models are \"DN15\" and \"DN20\", then Q3 has 2 values for DN15... can't represent one model with multiple values in the simple form.\n\nI could define models as combinations: model_id \"DN15-Q3=1.6\", \"DN15-Q3=2.5\", \"DN20-Q3=2.5\", \"DN20-Q3=4\". Then:\n- nominal_diameter: DN15→15, DN20→20 (each twice)\n- permanent_flowrate_q3: 1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 4\n- overload_flowrate_q4: 2, 3.125, 3.125, 5\n- pressure_loss: 0.16, 0.25, 0.16, 0.25... wait: DN15 Q3=1.6 → 0.16 bar; DN15 Q3=2.5 → 0.25; DN20 Q3=2.5 → 0.16; DN20 Q3=4 → 0.25. Yes.\n\nThen the ratio-dependent attributes (q1, q2, ratio Q3/Q1) vary within each model — that's a config axis \"ratio Q3/Q1\". Use config_level:\n- attribute: minimum_flowrate_q1, axis: \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", values: [{condition: \"DN15 Q3=1.6 ratio=80\", value: 0.020}, ...]\n\nThat's 20 entries for q1 and 20 for q2. Plus ratio entries... Actually ratio Q3/Q1 IS the axis condition.\n\nAlternatively, keep the full tables in matrix_tables with rows per variant, and populate model_level with the DN-level attributes only (nominal_diameter, connection_thread, construction_length), and config_level for flowrate-related things.\n\nLet me design matrix_tables to fully preserve Tab 1 and Tab 2:\n\nmatrix_tables[0]:\n- name: \"Tab. 1 — Technical and metrological data, DN15\"\n- columns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"connection_thread\", \"construction_length\", \"installation_position\", \"water_temperature_range\", \"meter_temperature_class\", \"maximum_working_pressure\", \"pressure_loss\", \"mpe_upper_flowrates\", \"mpe_lower_flowrates\", \"scale_interval\", \"capacity_of_calculator\", \"accuracy_class\", \"mechanical_class\", \"climatic_class\", \"electromagnetic_class\", \"environmental_classification\", \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", \"battery\"]\n\nHmm, that's very wide. Rows: 10 variants (5 for Q3=1.6, 5 for Q3=2.5). Each row: q1, q2, ratio. Other fields constant.\n\nActually, the rows here are columns in the original table (the original table is transposed: attributes as rows, variants as columns). Rows in my matrix:\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 15, \"permanent_flowrate_q3\": 1.6, \"minimum_flowrate_q1\": 0.020, \"transitional_flowrate_q2\": 0.032, \"overload_flowrate_q4\": 2, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 80, \"ratio_q2_q1\": 1.6, \"pressure_loss\": 0.16, ...}\n\nThat's a good \"long\" format: one row per variant. 10 rows for Tab 1, 9 rows for Tab 2.\n\nThen model_level: key attributes with values per model. If models are the 4 DN×Q3 combos, then:\n- nominal_diameter\n- permanent_flowrate_q3\n- overload_flowrate_q4\n- pressure_loss\n- connection_thread (DN15: \"G 3/4\"; DN20: \"G 1\")\n- construction_length (list of values — hmm, this is a list: \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\". Per rule 2, lists of values... For construction length, it's a set of available lengths, not a matrix. I'd record as a string value or array? The rules say numbers not strings, but a list of available options... I'll record as string \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\" or as an array. The schema value must be number|minmax|string. Hmm. I could record as string \"80/105/110/115/165/170\" — or keep comma list. I'll use the string as-is with decimal fix: \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\". Or as an array of numbers — but schema says value: number|{min,max}|string. I'll use a string.\n\nWait — but if this is type_level (constant across models), fine. Construction length differs between DN15 and DN20. So model_level.\n\nThen config_level for q1/q2/ratio:\n- axis: \"ratio_q3_over_q1\" — for each model combination.\n\nConfig level format: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value, unit_symbol}]}. So:\n\n{\"attribute\": \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"axis\": \"model × ratio_q3_over_q1\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6 m³/h, ratio 80\", \"value\": 0.02}, ...]}\n\nHmm, this is getting heavy but it's faithful. Let me enumerate.\n\nDN15, Q3=1.6:\n- ratio 80: Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032\n- ratio 160: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016\n- ratio 250: Q1=0.0064, Q2=0.010\n- ratio 315: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008\n- ratio 400: Q1=0.004, Q2=0.0064\n\nDN15, Q3=2.5:\n- ratio 80: Q1=0.031, Q2=0.050\n- ratio 160: Q1=0.0156, Q2=0.025\n- ratio 250: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016\n- ratio 400: Q1=0.0062, Q2=0.010\n- ratio 800: Q1=0.0031, Q2=0.005\n\nDN20, Q3=2.5:\n- ratio 80: Q1=0.031, Q2=0.05\n- ratio 160: Q1=0.0156, Q2=0.025\n- ratio 250: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016\n- ratio 400: Q1=0.0062, Q2=0.010\n\nDN20, Q3=4:\n- ratio 80: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080\n- ratio 160: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040\n- ratio 250: Q1=0.016, Q2=0.026\n- ratio 400: Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016\n- ratio 800: Q1=0.005, Q2=0.008\n\nNote DN20 Q3=2.5 has only 4 ratio options (80,160,250,400) — no 800.\n\nDecimal conversions: 1,6 → 1.6; 2,5 → 2.5; 0,020 → 0.02; 0,010 → 0.01; 0,0064 → 0.0064; 0,005 → 0.005; 0,004 → 0.004; 0,031 → 0.031; 0,0156 → 0.0156; 0,0062 → 0.0062; 0,0031 → 0.0031; 0,032 → 0.032; 0,016 → 0.016; 0,008 → 0.008; 0,050 → 0.05; 0,025 → 0.025; 0,005 → 0.005; 2 → 2; 3,125 → 3.125; 1,6 → 1.6 (ratio Q2/Q1); 0,16 → 0.16; 0,25 → 0.25; 0,001 → 0.001; 999999,999 → 999999.999; -15 to +70 → {min:-15, max:70}; 3,6 V → 3.6 V.\n\nWater temperature range: multiple options (0,1 to 30; 0,1 to 50; 30 to 70; 0,1 to 70) corresponding to temperature classes T30, T50, T30/70, T70. These are configuration options. I could record as config_level with axis \"meter_temperature_class\":\n- {condition: \"T30\", value: {min:0.1, max:30}, unit: \"°C\"}\n- {condition: \"T50\", value: {min:0.1, max:50}}\n- {condition: \"T30/70\", value: {min:30, max:70}}\n- {condition: \"T70\", value: {min:0.1, max:70}}\n\nThat's nice. Same for both tables.\n\nType-level characteristics (constant across all models in this fragment):\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6, dimensionless\n- installation_position: \"Horizontal / Vertical\"\n- water_temperature_range: config-level (per temperature class) — or type level as list of ranges. I'll do config_level per temp class.\n- meter_temperature_class: [\"T30\",\"T50\",\"T30/70\",\"T70\"] — hmm, value would be a string list. Could record as type_level with value \"T30 / T50 / T30/70 / T70\"? Or omit since captured in config axis. I'll record water_temperature_range as config-level and skip separate temperature class entry, or include it as a string list. Let me include temperature class options... Actually I'll handle via config axis and note in matrix tables.\n- maximum_working_pressure: 16 bar\n- mpe_upper: ±2% at Θ≤30°C, ±3% at Θ>30°C — config-level with axis \"water_temperature\". values: [{condition: \"Θ ≤ 30 °C\", value: -2..+2}?] Hmm, ±2 is a tolerance ±2%. Value as {min: -2, max: 2}, unit \"%\". condition \"at Θ ≤ 30 °C\". And {condition: \"at Θ > 30 °C\", value: {min:-3, max:3}, unit \"%\"}.\n- mpe_lower: ±5 → {min:-5, max:5}, unit \"%\".\n- scale_interval: 0.001 m³\n- capacity_of_calculator: 999999.999 m³\n- accuracy_class: 2 (dimensionless)\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: {min:-15, max:70} °C\n- electromagnetic_class: \"E2\"\n- environmental_classification: \"B/O\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- battery: li-battery 3.6 V, life 16 years — split: battery_voltage: 3.6 V; battery_life: 16 years? battery type \"li-battery\". I'll record battery_voltage 3.6 V and battery_type \"Li\", battery_lifetime 16 a. Keep simple: battery: \"li-battery 3.6 V, life time 16 years\" as string, plus structured battery_voltage.\n\nThe accountable alarms section 2.5: free text. Could go into certified_type.description? But this fragment doesn't include the type designation context... Actually Tab 1 and Tab 2 both say Type/model: QALCOSONIC W1. So certified_type can be populated: category \"Water meter\" (R49 is water meters; category example list includes \"Gas meter\" etc. — \"Water meter\" fits), type_designations: [\"QALCOSONIC W1\"], module_designation: not mentioned → omit or null. description: maybe the software note \"The software version is indicated on the data plate in the form SW:1.03 (SW: 2.02).\" Hmm, description is \"free-text description if present\" — the fragment doesn't have a general description. I could skip description. But software info: I'll capture in matrix_tables \"Software versions\".\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1\"\n- scheme: \"A\" (the A in the number)\n- page_total: 12 (from page footer \"of 12 pages\")\n- project_number: \"TK-P05-85\"? That's the Slovak form number. Schema has project_number field. I'll put \"TK-P05-85\". Hmm, is that a project number? It looks like an SLM internal form/procedure number. I'll include it as project_number — risky. Maybe better in issuing_authority? No field. I'll include it since certificate.project_number exists and this is the closest match.\n\nActually, be careful: the footer \"TK-P05-85 — Page 4 of 12 pages — V03/23-01-10\" is a form identifier. project_number: \"TK-P05-85\" seems reasonable.\n\n- member_state: Slovakia (from \"SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY\" stamp). The stamp indicates issuing authority is in Slovakia. member_state: \"Slovakia\". Reasonable.\n\n- date_issued: not in fragment. Omit.\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology\" (from stamp). \n- No address. person etc. not present.\n\nShould I include issuing_authority from a stamp? The stamp says \"SLOVAK LEGAL METROLOGY\" with SLM logo. It's a legitimate extraction. I'll include name only.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\"\n- edition: 2013\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [\"2\"]\n\nHmm — accuracy class 2 for water meters. R49 water meter accuracy classes are 1 and 2. So [\"2\"].\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"QALCOSONIC W1\"]\n- module_designation: omit (null)\n- description: I might include a short description combining: \"Ultrasonic water meter...\" — no, don't fabricate. Omit.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"QALCOSONIC W1\"\n- models: [\n  {\"model_id\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6\", \"label\": \"DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": 15, \"permanent_flowrate_q3\": 1.6, \"overload_flowrate_q4\": 2, \"connection_thread\": \"G 3/4\", \"pressure_loss\": 0.16}},\n  {\"model_id\": \"DN15-Q3=2.5\", ...},\n  {\"model_id\": \"DN20-Q3=2.5\", ...},\n  {\"model_id\": \"DN20-Q3=4\", ...}\n]\n\nWait, but should models be at DN level with the fragment noting other DNs appear in later parts (software table mentions DN25, DN32, DN40)? The software table remarks DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32 → SW 1.03; DN40 → SW 2.02. Those DNs are part of this fragment. But their metrological tables are in other fragments. I could note the DN list. For models, I'll only create models from the tables present (DN15, DN20). The software matrix table records the DN→SW mapping.\n\nHmm, actually maybe simpler: model_id = \"DN15\" and \"DN20\" with attributes for the two Q3 variants... No — Q3 differs. Combination model ids are cleaner.\n\nAlternatively model_id \"DN15/Q3=1.6\" etc. Let me use \"DN15-Q3=1.6\" format.\n\nThen config_level for q1/q2 per ratio:\n\ncharacteristics.config_level: [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"minimum_flowrate_q1\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\",\n    \"axis\": \"ratio_q3_over_q1\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6 ratio 80\", \"value\": 0.02},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6 ratio 160\", \"value\": 0.01},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6 ratio 250\", \"value\": 0.0064},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6 ratio 315\", \"value\": 0.005},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6 ratio 400\", \"value\": 0.004},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN15-Q3=2.5 ratio 80\", \"value\": 0.031},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN15-Q3=2.5 ratio 160\", \"value\": 0.0156},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN15-Q3=2.5 ratio 250\", \"value\": 0.01},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN15-Q3=2.5 ratio 400\", \"value\": 0.0062},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN15-Q3=2.5 ratio 800\", \"value\": 0.0031},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN20-Q3=2.5 ratio 80\", \"value\": 0.031},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN20-Q3=2.5 ratio 160\", \"value\": 0.0156},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN20-Q3=2.5 ratio 250\", \"value\": 0.01},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN20-Q3=2.5 ratio 400\", \"value\": 0.0062},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN20-Q3=4 ratio 80\", \"value\": 0.05},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN20-Q3=4 ratio 160\", \"value\": 0.025},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN20-Q3=4 ratio 250\", \"value\": 0.016},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN20-Q3=4 ratio 400\", \"value\": 0.01},\n      {\"condition\": \"DN20-Q3=4 ratio 800\", \"value\": 0.005}\n    ]\n  },\n  same for transitional_flowrate_q2\n]\n\nEach value should be structured {condition, value, unit_symbol}? The schema says values follow {condition, value, unit_symbol} — value itself is number/string per structured rule? Rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol/footnote_markers. But for matrix entries, the example shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — flat, no unit object; unit at attribute level. So for config_level, follow: {\"condition\": ..., \"value\": 0.02} with unit_symbol at attribute level. I'll put unit_symbol at the attribute-entry level (like model_level entries which have unit_symbol at attribute level). Wait, does model_level entry have unit_symbol at attribute level? In the R137 example: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, ...]}. Yes. So config_level similarly: {\"attribute\": ..., \"axis\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": ..., \"value\": ...}]}.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- nominal_diameter: DN15-Q3=1.6 → 15 (mm), DN15-Q3=2.5 → 15, DN20-Q3=2.5 → 20, DN20-Q3=4 → 20. unit mm.\n- permanent_flowrate_q3: 1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 4 (m³/h)\n- overload_flowrate_q4: 2, 3.125, 3.125, 5 (m³/h)\n- pressure_loss: 0.16, 0.25, 0.16, 0.25 (bar) — attribute name \"pressure_loss\" or \"pressure_loss_class\"? Table says \"Pressure loss class ΔP\". I'll use pressure_loss.\n- connection_thread: \"G 3/4\" for DN15 models, \"G 1\" for DN20 models. Value string.\n- construction_length: DN15: \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\" mm; DN20: \"105, 110, 130, 165, 190\" mm. unit mm, value string (list). Hmm, rule says lists of values are model matrices, not list-as-value... but that's for Emax-type numeric ranges of a single parameter. Construction length is genuinely a set of available lengths. Options: record as array? Schema restricts value to number|{min,max}|string. I'll use the string \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\". Fine.\n\ntype_level characteristics:\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6\n- installation_position: \"Horizontal / Vertical\" — value string. Maybe split into \"horizontal\" and \"vertical\"? Keep as string.\n- maximum_working_pressure_map: 16 bar\n- maximum_permissible_error_upper: config-level by temperature. Actually put in config_level with axis \"water_temperature\":\n  {\"attribute\": \"maximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrates\", \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", \"axis\": \"water_temperature\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"Θ ≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}}, {\"condition\": \"Θ > 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -3, \"max\": 3}}]}\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_flowrates: {min:-5, max:5} % — type_level with value {min:-5,max:5}. Rule: ranges become {min,max}. ±5 → {min:-5, max:5}. Yes.\n- scale_interval: 0.001 m³\n- capacity_of_calculator: 999999.999 m³\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: hmm \"Climatic class °C: -15 to +70\" → value {min:-15,max:70} unit °C. Label: climatic_class_temperature_range. I'll name \"climatic_class\" with value range and unit °C.\n- electromagnetic_class: \"E2\"\n- environmental_classification: \"B/O\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- battery_type: \"Li\" / battery: \"li-battery 3.6 V, life time 16 years\" — I'll do battery_voltage: 3.6 V and battery_lifetime: 16 years (\"a\"). Unit \"years\"? unit_symbol \"a\" or \"years\". I'll use \"years\". Hmm, keep raw: unit_symbol null, value \"life time 16 years\"? Better structured: battery_voltage {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, battery_lifetime {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"years\"}. Fine.\n- water_temperature_range per temperature class → config_level axis \"meter_temperature_class\":\n  {\"attribute\": \"water_temperature_range\", \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"axis\": \"meter_temperature_class\", \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"T30\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 30}},\n    {\"condition\": \"T50\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}},\n    {\"condition\": \"T30/70\", \"value\": {\"min\": 30, \"max\": 70}},\n    {\"condition\": \"T70\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 70}}\n  ]}\n\nNote Tab 1 has OCR artifact \"0,1 to.70\" for T70 — that's \"0,1 to 70\" → {min:0.1, max:70}.\n\nmatrix_tables: preserve Tab 1 and Tab 2 in long format plus software table.\n\nmatrix_tables:\n1. name: \"Software versions\", columns: [\"software_version\", \"checksum\", \"remarks\"], rows: [{software_version: \"1.03\", checksum: \"C983\", remarks: \"DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\"}, {software_version: \"2.02\", checksum: \"CD00\", remarks: \"DN40\"}]. description: \"The software version is indicated on the data plate in the form SW:1.03 (SW: 2.02). The checksum is indicated on the data plate in the form C983 (CD00).\"\n\n2. name: \"Tab. 1 — Technical and metrological data, DN15\", columns: [...], rows: 10 rows.\n\nColumns for Tab 1 long-format: model_id, permanent_flowrate_q3, minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, overload_flowrate_q4, ratio_q3_q1, ratio_q2_q1, pressure_loss. Plus column_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null, \"bar\"].\n\nRows Tab 1:\n{model_id: \"DN15-Q3=1.6\", q3: 1.6, q1: 0.020, q2: 0.032, q4: 2, ratio_q3_q1: 80, ratio_q2_q1: 1.6, pressure_loss: 0.16}\n{... ratio 160: q1 0.010, q2 0.016}\n{ratio 250: 0.0064, 0.010}\n{ratio 315: 0.005, 0.008}\n{ratio 400: 0.004, 0.0064}\n{model_id: \"DN15-Q3=2.5\", q3: 2.5, q4: 3.125, pressure_loss 0.25}\n{ratio 80: 0.031, 0.050}\n{ratio 160: 0.0156, 0.025}\n{ratio 250: 0.010, 0.016}\n{ratio 400: 0.0062, 0.010}\n{ratio 800: 0.0031, 0.005}\n\nRows Tab 2:\n{model_id: \"DN20-Q3=2.5\", q3 2.5, q4 3.125, pl 0.16}\n{ratio 80: 0.031, 0.05}\n{ratio 160: 0.0156, 0.025}\n{ratio 250: 0.010, 0.016}\n{ratio 400: 0.0062, 0.010}\n{model_id: \"DN20-Q3=4\", q3 4, q4 5, pl 0.25}\n{ratio 80: 0.050, 0.080}\n{ratio 160: 0.025, 0.040}\n{ratio 250: 0.016, 0.026}\n{ratio 400: 0.010, 0.016}\n{ratio 800: 0.005, 0.008}\n\nHmm wait — DN20 Q3=4 ratio 250: Q2 = 0.026? That seems odd (0.016×1.6=0.0256). The table says 0,026. Keep 0.026 as given.\n\nActually, should I duplicate everything in both matrix_tables and model_level/config_level? The rules say for R137: \"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". So yes, duplication is intended. OK.\n\nBut maybe I don't need to fully duplicate the q1/q2 ratios in both config_level and matrix_tables. Given the guidance, I'll include both but keep matrix tables complete and model_level/config_level as the key structured extract. Actually, to reduce redundancy risk, I could put ratio-dependent values only in matrix_tables and config_level... The instruction explicitly wants model_level populated too. I'll do it.\n\nLet me reconsider model granularity: maybe simpler to have models = \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R80\" etc. (20 models)? No — too granular; ratio is a config axis. 4 models with ratio config axis is good.\n\nSoftware table remarks also mention DN25, DN32, DN40 — these DNs exist in the cert (other fragments). Should models include them? I only have metrological data for DN15/DN20 here. The task: \"Return one JSON object ... for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" The software table is content in this fragment. I'll note in matrix_tables. I won't create models for DN25/32/40 since no metrological data here.\n\nNow, the certificate number and revision: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1\". revision_history: revision \"1\", date null, changes null? Don't fabricate — omit revision_history (no changes text). Actually could put revision \"1\" with no changes... Rule 8: don't fabricate. The Rev. 1 indicates revision 1 but no change description. I'd omit revision_history.\n\ncertificate.number: full \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1\". Maybe include a \"revision\": not in schema. Keep full number in number field.\n\ntest_reports: none in fragment.\n\nfootnotes: none.\n\ncomponents: none — the calculator/indication device is mentioned (\"the calculator and indication device detects automatically if a fault condition occurs\") but not as named components. Skip components.\n\nSection 2.5 accountable alarms: free text — could add to certified_type.description? The description field: \"free-text description if present\". There's no general description of the type in this fragment. I could omit. But the alarm behavior is a functional characteristic. Hmm. I could add a type_level characteristic \"accountable_alarm\": {value: \"automatically detects fault conditions during measurement, may stop the measurement and report an alarm indication on the display\"}. That seems reasonable to preserve. I'll add description to certified_type? Let me add it as certified_type.description — no, that's for describing the type. I'll put a type_level characteristic maybe not... Let me keep it simple and skip, or include a brief description. I think including a \"accountable_alarms\" type_level string characteristic is reasonable and lossless. I'll include it.\n\nAlso the software note: \"The software version is indicated on the data plate in the form SW:1.03 (SW: 2.02).\" — captured in matrix_tables description.\n\nNow unit_id: leave null everywhere (not certain of unitsdb IDs). Actually could use \"u:degree_Celsius\" for °C as shown in example — but rule 3 says leave unit_id null unless certain. The example in rule 2 shows \"u:degree_Celsius\" as a possible value. I'll set unit_id null everywhere to be safe. Hmm, the schema example explicitly lists \"u:degree_Celsius\" — I'm fairly certain degree_Celsius exists in unitsdb. But to be safe and consistent, null everywhere. Actually the rule says \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain of the unitsdb ID.\" I'm reasonably certain about u:degree_Celsius being the pattern shown. But I'll just use null for all to minimize risk.\n\nNow let me write the attribute labels (snake_case, no LaTeX):\n- nominal_diameter\n- permanent_flowrate_q3\n- minimum_flowrate_q1\n- transitional_flowrate_q2\n- overload_flowrate_q4\n- ratio_q3_q1\n- ratio_q2_q1\n- connection_thread\n- construction_length\n- installation_position\n- water_temperature_range\n- meter_temperature_class\n- maximum_working_pressure_map\n- pressure_loss\n- maximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrates (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4)\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_flowrates (Q1 ≤ Q < Q2)\n- scale_interval\n- capacity_of_calculator\n- accuracy_class\n- mechanical_class\n- climatic_class (temperature range) → maybe \"climatic_class_temperature_range\"\n- electromagnetic_class\n- environmental_classification\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class\n- battery_voltage\n- battery_lifetime\n\nFor the battery row \"li-battery 3,6 V, life time 16 years\": battery_voltage 3.6 V; battery_lifetime 16 years; battery type \"li\" — maybe battery_chemistry: \"Li\". I'll add battery_type: \"Li\".\n\nMPE: value ±2% → {min:-2, max:2}. Keep conditions as strings \"Θ ≤ 30 °C\". The Θ is fine as a symbol? It's not LaTeX. OK. Maybe write \"at water temperature Θ ≤ 30 °C\".\n\nNow the models' attributes within model_family.models — include the model_level attributes as a convenience? The schema: models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. I'll include attributes per model: nominal_diameter, permanent_flowrate_q3, overload_flowrate_q4, pressure_loss, connection_thread, construction_length. And then model_level also lists these... duplication again but per schema design that's fine. Actually, to reduce bloat, maybe put attributes only in model_level and keep models[] minimal with model_id and label. The R60 example shows models entries with only model_id. I'll include model_id + label + minimal attributes (nominal_diameter). Hmm. Let me include the small set of distinguishing attributes in models[].attributes — it makes the JSON self-contained. But then model_level duplicates... The schema intends model_level to be the attribute×model matrix. I'll keep models[] light: model_id, label. And put all varying attributes in model_level. \n\nActually wait — construction_length: it's per-DN (DN15: six lengths; DN20: five lengths). In model_level with 4 model ids, DN15-Q3=1.6 and DN15-Q3=2.5 share the same value. That's fine.\n\nLet me now also decide: config_level entries needed:\n1. minimum_flowrate_q1 (axis: ratio_q3_over_q1 within model) — 20 values.\n2. transitional_flowrate_q2 — 20 values.\n3. water_temperature_range (axis: meter_temperature_class) — 4 values.\n4. maximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrates (axis: water_temperature) — 2 values.\n\nAlso ratio_q3_q1 itself is the axis condition, no separate entry needed.\n\nAlternatively, fold q1/q2 into matrix_tables only and skip config_level for them. But rule 5 encourages config_level for such. I'll include.\n\nShould the condition include the model id? Yes — axis = \"model × ratio_q3_over_q1\", condition strings like \"DN15-Q3=1.6 / R80\". I'll write condition as \"DN15-Q3=1.6, ratio Q3/Q1 = 80\".\n\nHmm, that makes conditions verbose. Fine.\n\nAlternatively, treat ratio variants as separate models after all... No, keep config approach.\n\nLet me also handle \"Construction length L\" values: DN15: 80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170. As string. unit mm.\n\n\"Connection thread\": \"G ¾\" → \"G 3/4\" (avoid the ¾ glyph? It's fine Unicode, but ASCII safer: \"G 3/4\"). I'll use \"G 3/4\" and \"G 1\".\n\nInstallation position: \"Horizontal / Vertical\" — type_level.\n\nWater temp range OCR artifact in Tab 1: \"0,1 to.70\" — treat as 0.1 to 70.\n\nMeter temperature class: T30/T50/T30/70/T70 — this maps to the water temp range config. I'll not list separately (it's the axis). Actually, maybe include type_level meter_temperature_classes as \"T30, T50, T30/70, T70\"? The config axis condition covers it. Skip.\n\nNow, certificate page_total: 12. Include.\n\nAlso include in certificate: member_state \"Slovakia\".\n\nproject_number: \"TK-P05-85\" — include. Hmm, \"TK-P05-85\" might be the test procedure/form. Given schema has project_number, include it. Also \"V03/23-01-10\" is form version/date — skip.\n\nissuing_authority: name \"Slovak Legal Metrology\". Include website? Not present. person_responsible? Not present. I'll include just name. Should I include given it's from a stamp? Yes.\n\nrecommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"2\"]. Hmm, numeric or string? accuracy_classes[] — class designations, string \"2\" fine. Or 2. I'll use \"2\".\n\nWait — should recommendation even be emitted given it's derived from cert number? Yes, R49 is clearly the recommendation. Edition 2013. Amendment: not present → omit. scheme \"A\".\n\nNow, is scheme A correct? \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02\" — the \"-A-\" indicates OIML certificate scheme A (type approval). Yes.\n\nLet me now also double check the Tab 2 Q2/Q1 ratio: 1.6 — yes.\n\nTab 2 DN20 Q3=2.5 has 4 ratio columns (80,160,250,400) — the Q1 row for Q3=2.5 part has 4 values: 0.031, 0.0156, 0.010, 0.0062. Yes (colspan 4). And Q3=4 part has 5 columns.\n\nTab 1 DN15 Q3=1.6 ratios: 80, 160, 250, 315, 400. Note 315 (unusual, but given). Q3=2.5: 80, 160, 250, 400, 800 (no 315). Good.\n\nNow build the JSON.\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"TK-P05-85\",\n    \"page_total\": 12,\n    \"member_state\": \"Slovakia\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Slovak Legal Metrology\"\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [\"2\"]\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"QALCOSONIC W1\"]\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"QALCOSONIC W1\",\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6\", \"label\": \"QALCOSONIC W1 DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN15-Q3=2.5\", \"label\": \"QALCOSONIC W1 DN15, Q3 = 2.5 m³/h\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN20-Q3=2.5\", \"label\": \"QALCOSONIC W1 DN20, Q3 = 2.5 m³/h\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN20-Q3=4\", \"label\": \"QALCOSONIC W1 DN20, Q3 = 4 m³/h\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"ratio_q2_q1\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"installation_position\": {\"value\": \"Horizontal / Vertical\", ...},\n      \"maximum_working_pressure\": {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\", ...},\n      \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_flowrates\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -5, \"max\": 5}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n      \"scale_interval\": {\"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      \"capacity_of_calculator\": {\"value\": 999999.999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": 2, ...},\n      \"mechanical_class\": {\"value\": \"M1\"},\n      \"climatic_class_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -15, \"max\": 70}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      \"electromagnetic_class\": {\"value\": \"E2\"},\n      \"environmental_classification\": {\"value\": \"B/O\"},\n      \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U0D0\"},\n      \"battery_type\": {\"value\": \"Li\"},\n      \"battery_voltage\": {\"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n      \"battery_lifetime\": {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"years\"},\n      \"accountable_alarms\": {\"value\": \"During the measuring process the calculator and indication device detects automatically if a fault condition occurs and eventually stops the measurement reporting an alarm indication on the display\"}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"nominal_diameter\", \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6\", \"value\": 15}, {\"model\": \"DN15-Q3=2.5\", \"value\": 15}, {\"model\": \"DN20-Q3=2.5\", \"value\": 20}, {\"model\": \"DN20-Q3=4\", \"value\": 20}]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 4]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [2, 3.125, 3.125, 5]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"pressure_loss\", \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\", \"values\": [0.16, 0.25, 0.16, 0.25]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"connection_thread\", \"values\": [\"G 3/4\", \"G 3/4\", \"G 1\", \"G 1\"]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"construction_length\", \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6\", \"value\": \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\"}, ...]}\n    ],\n    \"config_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"axis\": \"model × ratio_q3_over_q1\", \"values\": [20 entries]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", ...},\n      {\"attribute\": \"water_temperature_range\", \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"axis\": \"meter_temperature_class\", \"values\": [4 entries]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"maximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrates\", \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", \"axis\": \"water_temperature\", \"values\": [2 entries]}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...]\n}\n\nShould footnote_markers be included even when empty? I'll include only when non-empty, or omit entirely. Rule: structured values MUST be object with value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. Hmm — \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". I'll include all four keys consistently (footnote_markers: [] when none). That's verbose but compliant. For matrix entries ({model, value}) the example doesn't include unit objects. OK.\n\nFor config_level values: {\"condition\": \"...\", \"value\": ...} — the rule 5 says \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". So config values include unit_symbol? \"same structured form\" — ambiguous. The R137 model_level example omits per-value unit. I'll include unit_symbol at the attribute level only, and values as {condition, value}. That matches the example style.\n\nLet me write out the matrix_tables fully.\n\nTable: Software versions.\n{\n  \"name\": \"Software versions\",\n  \"columns\": [\"software_version\", \"checksum\", \"applicable_diameters\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"software_version\": \"1.03\", \"checksum\": \"C983\", \"applicable_diameters\": \"DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"2.02\", \"checksum\": \"CD00\", \"applicable_diameters\": \"DN40\"}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"The software version is indicated on the data plate in the form SW:1.03 (SW: 2.02). The checksum is indicated on the data plate in the form C983 (CD00).\"\n}\n\nTable: Tab. 1 (DN15):\n{\n  \"name\": \"Tab. 1 — Technical and metrological data, QALCOSONIC W1 DN15\",\n  \"columns\": [\"model_id\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"pressure_loss\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null, \"bar\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\": 1.6, \"minimum_flowrate_q1\": 0.020, \"transitional_flowrate_q2\": 0.032, \"overload_flowrate_q4\": 2, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 80, \"ratio_q2_q1\": 1.6, \"pressure_loss\": 0.16},\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6\", ..., \"ratio_q3_q1\": 160, q1 0.010, q2 0.016},\n    {ratio 250: 0.0064, 0.010},\n    {ratio 315: 0.005, 0.008},\n    {ratio 400: 0.004, 0.0064},\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN15-Q3=2.5\", q3 2.5, ratio 80: 0.031, 0.050, q4 3.125, pl 0.25},\n    {ratio 160: 0.0156, 0.025},\n    {ratio 250: 0.010, 0.016},\n    {ratio 400: 0.0062, 0.010},\n    {ratio 800: 0.0031, 0.005}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Other constant attributes: connection thread G 3/4; construction length 80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170 mm; installation horizontal/vertical; water temperature ranges 0.1–30 °C (T30), 0.1–50 °C (T50), 30–70 °C (T30/70), 0.1–70 °C (T70); MAP 16 bar; MPE upper ±2% (Θ ≤ 30 °C) / ±3% (Θ > 30 °C); MPE lower ±5%; scale interval 0.001 m³; calculator capacity 999999.999 m³; accuracy class 2; M1; climatic -15/+70 °C; E2; B/O; U0D0; Li battery 3.6 V, 16 years.\"\n}\n\nHmm — description could just be \"Transposed table; original attributes as rows.\" Keep description shorter: note it's transposed and DN = 15 mm. I'll include a brief description: \"DN15; table transposed (one column per ratio variant). Constant attributes: ...\". Actually the constant attributes are already captured in type_level/model_level/config_level, so description can be minimal. I'll keep a brief one.\n\nTable: Tab. 2 (DN20) — similar with 9 rows.\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Tab. 2 — Technical and metrological data, QALCOSONIC W1 DN20\",\n  \"columns\": [same],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN20-Q3=2.5\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\": 2.5, \"minimum_flowrate_q1\": 0.031, \"transitional_flowrate_q2\": 0.05, \"overload_flowrate_q4\": 3.125, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 80, \"ratio_q2_q1\": 1.6, \"pressure_loss\": 0.16},\n    {160: 0.0156, 0.025},\n    {250: 0.010, 0.016},\n    {400: 0.0062, 0.010},\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN20-Q3=4\", q3 4, ratio 80: 0.050, 0.080, q4 5, pl 0.25},\n    {160: 0.025, 0.040},\n    {250: 0.016, 0.026},\n    {400: 0.010, 0.016},\n    {800: 0.005, 0.008}\n  ]\n}\n\nNow write config_level values fully.\n\nminimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h), axis \"model × ratio_q3_over_q1\":\nDN15-Q3=1.6: 80→0.02, 160→0.01, 250→0.0064, 315→0.005, 400→0.004\nDN15-Q3=2.5: 80→0.031, 160→0.0156, 250→0.01, 400→0.0062, 800→0.0031\nDN20-Q3=2.5: 80→0.031, 160→0.0156, 250→0.01, 400→0.0062\nDN20-Q3=4: 80→0.05, 160→0.025, 250→0.016, 400→0.01, 800→0.005\n\ntransitional_flowrate_q2:\nDN15-Q3=1.6: 80→0.032, 160→0.016, 250→0.01, 315→0.008, 400→0.0064\nDN15-Q3=2.5: 80→0.05, 160→0.025, 250→0.016, 400→0.01, 800→0.005\nDN20-Q3=2.5: 80→0.05, 160→0.025, 250→0.016, 400→0.01\nDN20-Q3=4: 80→0.08, 160→0.04, 250→0.026, 400→0.016, 800→0.008\n\nNumber formatting: 0.020 → 0.02 in JSON (JSON numbers can't preserve trailing zeros). That's fine.\n\nwater_temperature_range (°C), axis \"meter_temperature_class\":\nT30 → {min: 0.1, max: 30}\nT50 → {min: 0.1, max: 50}\nT30/70 → {min: 30, max: 70}\nT70 → {min: 0.1, max: 70}\n\nmaximum_permissible_error_upper_flowrates (%), axis \"water_temperature\":\n\"Θ ≤ 30 °C\" → {min: -2, max: 2}\n\"Θ > 30 °C\" → {min: -3, max: 3}\n\nType level: maximum_working_pressure — Tab 1 says \"Maximum working pressure MAP\", Tab 2 \"Maximum working pressure\". Label: maximum_working_pressure_map? I'll use \"maximum_working_pressure\" for cleanliness, since MAP is in the name... canonical label rule: keep it simple. I'll use \"maximum_working_pressure_map\" to preserve MAP? Hmm, examples show shortening like \"Ratio of minimum LC Verification interval Y=Emax/vmin\" → ratio_y_emax_over_vmin. I'll use \"maximum_working_pressure\" (value 16 bar).\n\nbattery_lifetime unit: \"years\" — or \"a\" (annum). I'll use \"years\".\n\nAlso should I record meter_temperature_class as type_level list of options? The config axis covers it. Skip.\n\nOne more thing: \"Water temperature range\" options \"0,1 to 30\" → min 0.1. And \"30 to 70\" for T30/70.\n\nEverything set. Also certificate.date_issued absent — omit.\n\nNow let me also consider whether to include \"label\" for models. Schema: model_id, label, attributes{}. I'll include label with full description.\n\nShould attributes{} in models include key values? I'll add nominal_diameter and permanent_flowrate_q3 as simple values for convenience:\n{\"model_id\": \"DN15-Q3=1.6\", \"label\": \"...\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": {\"value\": 15, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}, ...}}? That triples duplication. Keep it minimal: include attributes with nominal_diameter, permanent_flowrate_q3, overload_flowrate_q4, pressure_loss, connection_thread, construction_length — as flat values? The schema