lude as type_level \"accountable_alarms\" with string value summarizing (keep close to original text).\n\nAlso the software indication on data plate: captured in matrix table description.\n\nOne more consideration — \"Ratio Q2/Q1\" type_level label: \"ratio_q2_q1\". Value 1.6 dimensionless.\n\n\"Accuracy class\" 2: value 2, unit null. Or string \"2\"? It's numeric class. Use 2. Hmm, class 2 as number is fine; recommendation.accuracy_classes [\"2\"]. Slight inconsistency but OK. Actually let me make accuracy_class value 2 (number).\n\nMechanical class M1, electromagnetic E2, environmental B/O, flow profile U0D0 — strings.\n\nConstruction length strings: \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\" and \"105, 110, 130, 165, 190\".\n\nNow assemble. I'll keep footnote_markers: [] in all structured values per the \"strict\" shape requirement. Actually that adds a lot of tokens... but compliance matters. The example in rule 7 shows footnote_markers only when referenced. Rule 2's shape includes footnote_markers in the object. I'll include \"footnote_markers\": [] everywhere in type_level. For model_level/config_level compact entries, examples omit them. OK.\n\nFinal JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {...},\n  \"issuing_authority\": {...},\n  \"recommendation\": {...},\n  \"certified_type\": {...},\n  \"model_family\": {...},\n  \"characteristics\": {\"type_level\": {...}, \"model_level\": [...], \"config_level\": [...]},\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...]\n}",
